WEBVTT

00:00:00.593 --> 00:00:01.550
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:04.430
Today, we are not picking up the scissors yet.

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:07.230
We are talking about what happens before the cut:

00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:11.950
the consultation, reading the hair, the shampoo, and sectioning.

00:00:12.427 --> 00:00:13.470
It sounds simple.

00:00:13.817 --> 00:00:17.150
But a lot of bad haircuts do not go wrong because of the scissors.

00:00:17.581 --> 00:00:19.522
They go wrong in the first five minutes,

00:00:19.472 --> 00:00:22.830
when the stylist and the client have not really understood each other.

00:00:23.484 --> 00:00:25.319
Here is something that happens all the time:

00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:27.879
the client says, just take a little off.

00:00:28.197 --> 00:00:30.839
To the client, a little means one centimeter.

00:00:31.108 --> 00:00:33.559
To the stylist, a little can mean five.

00:00:33.904 --> 00:00:38.519
After the cut, the client is unhappy, and the stylist has no idea what went wrong.

00:00:38.812 --> 00:00:42.919
So don't talk in words like a little, a lot, short or long.

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:46.657
Ask what the client likes and doesn't like about their hair right now,

00:00:46.607 --> 00:00:48.279
and ask them to show you a photo.

00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:53.008
Then agree on the length with a number, or with a point on the client's body:

00:00:53.279 --> 00:00:57.088
to the chin, touching the shoulders, level with the collarbone.

00:00:57.522 --> 00:01:01.408
Lift a section of hair, hold it right at that spot, and check again:

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:03.728
we are cutting to here, is that right?

00:01:04.308 --> 00:01:05.968
Ask about their lifestyle, too.

00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:09.745
Do they blow-dry at home, how long do they spend each morning,

00:01:09.695 --> 00:01:11.488
do they often tie their hair up,

00:01:11.673 --> 00:01:12.688
do they play sports?

00:01:12.796 --> 00:01:16.695
A style that looks great in the salon, but that the client can't do at home,

00:01:16.645 --> 00:01:19.328
is a style they won't like anymore a few days later.

00:01:19.988 --> 00:01:22.928
The next step is reading the hair, while it is still dry.

00:01:23.415 --> 00:01:26.128
First, the face shape and the shape of the head.

00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:28.848
Pull all the hair back and look at the face:

00:01:28.948 --> 00:01:32.208
round, long, square, or oval.

00:01:32.703 --> 00:01:37.711
The cutting line, the volume, and where the hair stops can make a face look longer,

00:01:37.661 --> 00:01:38.368
or rounder.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.128
Head shape matters too.

00:01:40.464 --> 00:01:42.688
Some people are quite flat at the back of the head.

00:01:42.948 --> 00:01:46.048
Leave more weight there, and the head looks rounder and nicer.

00:01:46.615 --> 00:01:47.898
Then the hair itself.

00:01:48.419 --> 00:01:49.098
Density:

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.058
make a parting, and see how much scalp shows.

00:01:52.324 --> 00:01:53.978
Is the hair thick or thin?

00:01:54.538 --> 00:01:55.258
Texture:

00:01:55.527 --> 00:01:58.378
fine, medium, or coarse strands.

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:00.858
Fine hair shows every scissor line;

00:02:01.206 --> 00:02:03.578
cut a little wrong, and you see it straight away.

00:02:04.006 --> 00:02:06.618
Coarse hair is stiff, and stands up easily.

00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:08.138
And the curl pattern:

00:02:08.347 --> 00:02:10.618
straight, wavy, or curly.

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.778
Each one needs a different way of cutting.

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:15.666
Something new stylists often overlook:

00:02:15.874 --> 00:02:16.866
the growth pattern.

00:02:17.426 --> 00:02:19.586
Almost everyone has a whorl at the crown.

00:02:20.114 --> 00:02:23.586
Many people have one at the nape, or a cowlick at the front hairline.

00:02:24.093 --> 00:02:27.106
In those places, the hair won't lie down the way you want.

00:02:27.534 --> 00:02:29.826
Cut it too short, and it stands straight up.

00:02:30.172 --> 00:02:33.294
So before you cut, comb the hair in several directions,

00:02:33.244 --> 00:02:35.026
and see how it falls naturally.

00:02:35.192 --> 00:02:38.744
Where there is a whorl, you usually leave the hair a little longer,

00:02:38.694 --> 00:02:40.626
or cut with the direction of growth.

00:02:41.155 --> 00:02:43.680
Wet hair and dry hair are different, too.

00:02:44.073 --> 00:02:48.720
When it is wet, the hair is heavy, it stretches out straight, and looks longer.

00:02:49.224 --> 00:02:53.360
Wavy and curly hair shrinks up as it dries, sometimes by a lot.

00:02:53.936 --> 00:02:58.080
A curl pulled straight to below the shoulders can end up at the chin once it is dry.

00:02:58.583 --> 00:03:02.405
So with curly hair, cut longer than you intend, or cut it dry,

00:03:02.355 --> 00:03:04.960
with a technique you have been trained in.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:05.920
Remember:

00:03:06.163 --> 00:03:10.240
you can always cut more, but hair that has been cut can't be put back.

00:03:10.838 --> 00:03:13.121
Before the shampoo, look at the scalp.

00:03:13.691 --> 00:03:19.822
If the scalp has an open wound, is inflamed, red and swollen, has pus, or has lice,

00:03:19.772 --> 00:03:21.361
don't do the service.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:25.841
Speak quietly and privately with the client, and suggest they see a doctor.

00:03:26.212 --> 00:03:28.241
Don't let them feel embarrassed in front of others.

00:03:28.621 --> 00:03:32.401
For more detail, watch the lesson on when not to treat a client again.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.159
Then the shampoo.

00:03:34.484 --> 00:03:37.199
For the client, this is the most relaxing moment.

00:03:37.548 --> 00:03:41.359
For the stylist, it is when the client judges whether you are careful or not.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:45.377
Test the water on your own wrist first, before it touches the client,

00:03:45.327 --> 00:03:47.439
and ask them if the temperature is okay.

00:03:47.833 --> 00:03:50.639
Put a towel around the shoulders, to keep the collar dry.

00:03:51.062 --> 00:03:54.959
The neck rests comfortably on the towel in the basin, with nothing pressing.

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:58.639
Massage the scalp with your fingertips, never your nails.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:02.186
Rinse thoroughly, with one hand shielding the hairline,

00:04:02.136 --> 00:04:04.719
so water doesn't run into the face and ears.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:10.240
After the shampoo, blot the hair with a towel, pressing gently, never rubbing.

00:04:10.728 --> 00:04:14.960
Rubbing hard tangles the hair, and roughens the cuticle of each strand.

00:04:15.374 --> 00:04:19.620
To detangle, start at the ends, then work gradually up to the roots,

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.040
with a wide-tooth comb.

00:04:21.458 --> 00:04:25.200
Hold the hair above with your other hand, so the client doesn't feel any pulling.

00:04:25.842 --> 00:04:27.204
Now, sectioning.

00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:29.364
Why do clean sections matter?

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:33.524
Because you can only cut precisely the hair that you can see and control.

00:04:33.868 --> 00:04:36.657
With straight partings and sections that are not too big,

00:04:36.607 --> 00:04:39.204
you can always find your previous cutting line again.

00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:42.004
The most common way is seven sections:

00:04:42.163 --> 00:04:46.676
the top, two side sections over the ears, two upper back sections,

00:04:46.626 --> 00:04:48.164
and two nape sections.

00:04:48.394 --> 00:04:51.844
Twist each section neatly, and clip it flat against the head.

00:04:52.102 --> 00:04:54.804
And keep the hair evenly damp, with a spray bottle.

00:04:55.473 --> 00:04:59.607
Finally, a few words that every cutting lesson after this one will use.

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:01.527
First, the guideline.

00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:03.526
That is your very first cut.

00:05:03.707 --> 00:05:05.527
Every cut after it follows it.

00:05:06.004 --> 00:05:07.447
Next, elevation:

00:05:07.775 --> 00:05:10.247
the angle at which you hold the hair away from the head.

00:05:10.827 --> 00:05:14.716
At 0°, the hair falls straight down, giving a one-length line,

00:05:14.666 --> 00:05:16.327
with weight at the bottom.

00:05:16.754 --> 00:05:20.487
At 45°, you get low layers that still keep their weight.

00:05:20.651 --> 00:05:24.026
At 90°, the hair is held at a right angle to the scalp,

00:05:23.976 --> 00:05:26.807
making even layers that follow the head shape.

00:05:27.156 --> 00:05:30.304
At 180°, it is lifted straight up to the top,

00:05:30.254 --> 00:05:33.127
and the hair below gets gradually longer,

00:05:33.158 --> 00:05:34.327
giving long layers.

00:05:35.016 --> 00:05:36.656
Then, over-direction:

00:05:37.159 --> 00:05:40.816
pulling the hair away from where it naturally falls, toward the guideline.

00:05:41.417 --> 00:05:43.035
Whichever way you over-direct,

00:05:42.985 --> 00:05:46.016
the hair gets gradually longer in the opposite direction.

00:05:46.572 --> 00:05:47.776
Then, tension.

00:05:48.417 --> 00:05:50.976
Keep an even, moderate pull on the hair.

00:05:51.485 --> 00:05:56.258
Pull too tight, especially on wet hair, curly hair, or around the ears,

00:05:56.208 --> 00:05:57.456
and once it dries,

00:05:57.723 --> 00:05:59.616
the hair springs up shorter than you wanted.

00:05:59.717 --> 00:06:01.456
And finally, cross-checking.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.044
Once you have cut in one direction, section the hair the opposite way to check it,

00:06:05.994 --> 00:06:08.016
then compare the left and right sides.

00:06:08.665 --> 00:06:11.534
So you see, a good haircut starts before the cut:

00:06:11.955 --> 00:06:16.733
listen carefully, look carefully, shampoo gently, and section cleanly.

00:06:17.069 --> 00:06:20.574
In the practice lesson, we will do it step by step, together.

00:06:20.916 --> 00:06:23.694
And now, please take the short quiz below the video.

00:06:24.034 --> 00:06:24.814
See you next time.
